# {{ twin_name }}: digital twin

A digital twin is a typed graph of a real system (objects, links between them, properties) together with every past version of that graph. This one is `{{ twin_id }}`. You work with it through one HTTP API.

Purpose, as agreed with its owner: {{ purpose }}

## How to reach it

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| API root | the value of the environment variable `{{ base_url_env }}`, followed by `/api/v1` |
| Token | the environment variable `{{ token_env }}`, sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every request |
| Machine-readable description of every endpoint | `GET /api/v1/openapi.json` |

- Never print, log or store the token. Pass it from the environment, as the recipes do.
- If either variable is unset, stop and ask the user for it. Do not guess an address.

## Start of every session

1. `GET /meta/health`: confirms the twin answers and gives its head version, its tier and its fidelity state{% if live %}, the twin's time and how old its newest data is{% endif %}.
2. `GET /meta/schema`: the twin's types, their properties, units and descriptions, and the link types.

Take type and property names from the schema, never from memory and never from this file: the schema is the only authority, and it can change between versions.

This skill was written from manifest `{{ short_hash }}`. If `manifest.hash` in `/meta/health` starts differently, the twin was revised after this skill was written: tell the user, and trust the twin's own answers over this file.

