## A live twin

`/meta/health` has two sections that matter here:

- `clock`: the twin's time (`now`) and where it is read from (`kind`). This is "now" for every question about the present, about how old something is, or about "the last hour". Use it, never your own clock: the twin's time is that of its sources. `available: false` means the twin cannot tell the time at the moment: say so, and do not answer questions about the present.
- `sync`: `interval` (how often the sources are read); `questions`, one entry per question that sets a limit on the age of its data: `staleness` (that limit), `freshness_s` (the age in seconds, on the twin's clock, of the newest data of the source the question waits for longest; only the sources the question is answered from count), `fresh` (whether the age is within the limit) and `source` (which source that is); `fresh`, `freshness_s` and `staleness` for the twin as a whole, which are those of the question nearest its limit; the state of each source; the last run; and the run in progress if there is one.

Before a contract answer about the present:

1. Read `/meta/health` and find the question in `sync.questions`. If its `fresh` is false, its data is older than was agreed: give the answer with its age and say that it is outside the agreed limit. Its `source` names the source that is behind; `GET /sync/sources` says why. A question that is not listed has no limit. `fresh: null` means the age cannot be measured now (the clock is unavailable, or nothing was read yet): say that.
2. Pin the head you read (`at=<head id>`). The next version can arrive while you work.

What the twin did, and why data is missing:

- `GET /sync/sources`: each source's status (`ok`, `failing`, `disabled`), the time of its newest data, its last error, and how far each of its datasets has been read.
- `GET /sync/runs`: every run, newest first, with the rows it read, wrote, set aside and found late. Status `partial` means some sources failed and the others were synced.
- `GET /sync/runs/{uid}/rejects`: the rows a run set aside, each with the rule it broke. They are not in the twin.

`POST /sync/runs` makes the twin read its sources now instead of at the next interval; it is the only request that brings new observed data. Use it when the user asks for the newest data, or after a source was mended. Do not call it in a loop: the schedule already does that. The answer is a job; wait for it as recipe 8 shows.

