## How to do things

Read `reference/recipes.md` before the first request of each kind. In short:

| To | Use |
|---|---|
| see one object, or list and filter objects of a type | `/objects/{type}`, `/objects/{type}/{uid}` |
| find an object when only part of its uid is known | `/objects?uid_like=` |
| see what an object is connected to | `/objects/{type}/{uid}/links`, `/graph/neighbors` |
| find how two objects are connected, over all links or only those that pass a condition | `/graph/paths`, with `edge_where` |
| cut out a part of the graph | `POST /graph/subgraph` |
| see how a property changed | `/objects/{type}/{uid}/history` |
| see what changed between two versions | `/history/diff` |
| pull whole tables for analysis or training | `/block/nodes/{type}`, `/block/edges/{edge_type}`, `/block/slice/{type}` as Arrow or Parquet |
| see when and why versions were written | `/history/versions` |
| see what each load or sync run did, and the rows it set aside | `/sync/runs`, `/sync/runs/{uid}/rejects` |
{% if live %}
| see whether the data is current, and the state of each source | `/meta/health` (`clock`, `sync`), `/sync/sources` |
| make the twin read its sources now | `POST /sync/runs`, then `/lifecycle/jobs/{id}` |
{% endif %}
| find objects of any type by uid or name | `/search` |
| ask a question it was built to answer | `GET /questions`, `POST /questions/{id}/answer` |
| run one of its functions with arguments | `GET /functions`, `POST /functions/{id}/run` |
| see its models and how well each forecast in its backtests | `GET /models`, `GET /models/{uid}/backtests` |
| forecast with a model | `POST /models/{uid}/predict` |
| evaluate the models again after new data | `POST /models/evaluate`, then `POST /questions/measure` |
| build a frame for training your own model | `POST /block/training-set` |
| see what the twin would do under a change | `POST /scenarios`, `POST /scenarios/{id}/run`, `GET /scenarios/{id}/compare` |
| keep values you worked out in the twin | `POST /derived` |
| read the twin's own logs | `/lifecycle/logs` |

All endpoints, with parameters: `reference/api.md`. Error codes and what to do about each: `reference/errors.md`.

## Rules

1. Read the schema before naming a type or property.
2. Pin the version for multi-step work.
3. Ask for tables as Arrow or Parquet when you need more than a few hundred rows and can read them. In JSON every table is paged (100 rows unless `limit`): follow `next_cursor` to the end before you count or conclude anything.
4. Let the twin do the filtering, the sorting, the counting, the traversal and the history: use `where`, `sort`, `count=true`, the graph endpoints with `edge_where`, and the slice endpoint instead of downloading everything and searching it yourself.
5. Every value has provenance (`include=provenance`): where it came from, whether it was observed, inferred, assumed or simulated, and when it was true. Quote it when the user asks how a number is known.
6. Report what the twin says. If the data does not answer a question, say that; do not fill the gap with an estimate presented as the twin's. A null is "no value held", never zero. If a definition leaves a choice open (how to integrate, what counts as an occurrence, which time zone a day is in), state the choice you made with the answer.
7. Reading never changes the twin, nor does asking a question or forecasting. Running a function changes it only when the function gives rows to write. What else changes it: registrations, evaluations and measurements, scenarios, derived data{% if live %} and sync runs (`POST /sync/runs`){% endif %} (`reference/api.md`, "What changes the twin"). Ask the user before any of these. Observed data cannot be edited through the API, and you should not try to reach the twin's database or its sources directly.
8. If the user needs something the contract does not cover (a new question, a tighter tolerance, another source), that is a revision of the contract: tell the user it has to be agreed and rebuilt; do not improvise it.
